//@ expect: accept
//@ reason: a bare `new Dog()` passed DIRECTLY as a call argument (no named
//@ reason: local in between) to a parameter declared as the BASE type must
//@ reason: construct the DERIVED type, not the parameter's declared type --
//@ reason: found as a real, general, pre-existing bug while developing
//@ reason: closures (a closure passed inline as a call argument is exactly
//@ reason: this shape: a `new`-like expression, not a named variable).
//@ reason: AstEmitter::emitMovedArg's own NewExpression branch used to call
//@ reason: `factoryFor(targetParam.first, ...)` -- passing the CALLEE'S
//@ reason: DECLARED parameter type (e.g. "Animal") as factoryFor's
//@ reason: CONSTRUCTED-type argument, discarding the actual `new
//@ reason: Expression::className` ("Dog") entirely. This produced
//@ reason: `useIt(std::make_shared<Animal>())` from source that says `new
//@ reason: Dog()` -- compiles, links, runs, and silently constructs the
//@ reason: WRONG CLASS, printing "generic" instead of "woof" (confirmed via
//@ reason: probe before the fix). Fixed by passing `neww->className` as
//@ reason: factoryFor's constructed-type argument and `targetParam.first` as
//@ reason: its declaredType argument instead -- the exact declared-vs-
//@ reason: constructed split factoryFor's own doc comment already describes
//@ reason: for a NAMED local (`Animal a = new Dog();`), now applied
//@ reason: correctly to the inline-argument case too. Must be RUN: this is
//@ reason: exactly the "compiles but does the wrong thing" class of bug
//@ reason: `-fsyntax-only` cannot catch.
//@ run: woof

class Animal {
public:
    Animal() { }
    virtual void speak() {
        std::cout << "generic" << std::endl;
    }
};

class Dog : public Animal {
public:
    Dog() { }
    void speak() override {
        std::cout << "woof" << std::endl;
    }
};

void useIt(Animal a) {
    a.speak();
}

int main() {
    useIt(new Dog());
    return 0;
}
